a new hydrogen factory off the i-15 and  apex exit in north las vegas is opening  up this month mary jane belliesa got a  tour of the facility she shows us how  the company is working to power zero  emission cars to reduce greenhouse gases  just miles off the motor speedway is the  air legate factory the company's goal is  to produce liquid hydrogen to supply the  transportation industry dave edwards the  director and advocate for hydrogen  energy at air lockheed says converting  to hydrogen for fuel is an energy saver  and will cut down on global warming so  if you think about those places in a  city like las vegas or in any any city  that has  heavy transit heavy transportation  sectors the particular emissions from  diesel from things like buses and from  trucks and and from cars themselves  those issues are addressed by battery  electric and fuel cell electric vehicles  because they have zero emissions at the  tailpipe the factory will convert  landfill gas and a purification process  to turn out low-cost low-carbon hydrogen  our production facility produces about  30 tons of hydrogen per day that would  be enough if you could take all of that  hydrogen and put it into fuel tanks for  light duty vehicles it would be on the  order of about 40 000 cars on the  highway currently around 20 people are  working at the plant they hope to expand  as this new market of transportation  fuel grows we know it's going to start  in california things usually do from a  transportation perspective if you've  seen anything from the california  markets you know that the shortage of  fuel and the the operation of the  stations has been limited by supply  chain and and so this should help  in a large degree to all of the the  consumers who are who are relying on  hydrogen for for day-to-day activities  and the startup of the north las vegas  plant is just the first step in  decarbonizing the transportation sector  as the company continues to expand here  in las vegas and across the 